K1, K2, K3, P1, P2 USD attribute <code>unreal:lensDistortion:K1</code> ... <code>P2</code> Default 0 Principal Point USD attribute <code>unreal:lensDistortion:PrincipalPoint</code> Default (0.5, 0.5) <p>UE's spherical lens model (Brown-Conrady), the values UE's Lens Distortion component and lens files take. K1 to K3 are radial, P1 and P2 tangential, all unitless. The principal point is the distortion centre, in normalized image coordinates. The Camera LOP has no parameter for them: author them with a Python LOP or in a USD layer:</p> <pre><code>over "camera"
{
    float unreal:lensDistortion:K1 = -0.2
}
</code></pre> <p>Distortion applies in both render modes and to every AOV except <code>depth</code>. Orthographic cameras are not distorted. USD's own <code>lensDistortion:*</code> attributes are not read: USD does not define the mathematics of its model.</p>

HoudiniUSD <ol> <li>Make sure the mesh's Subdivision Scheme (<code>subdivisionScheme</code>) is <code>catmullClark</code> (USD's default) or <code>loop</code>. <code>bilinear</code> and <code>none</code> render as authored.</li> <li>On hdUE Render Settings (Rendering tab, Show: Advanced), set Subdivision Level. The viewport's Display Options have it too, on their Unreal Engine tab.</li> <li>To give some meshes their own level, add a Render Geometry Settings LOP on them or an ancestor and set Subdivision Level on its Unreal Engine tab.</li> </ol> <p>Outside Houdini:</p> <pre><code>def RenderSettings "Settings"
{
    int unreal:subdivisionLevel = 3 # (1)!
}

def Mesh "hero"
{
    int primvars:unreal:subdivisionLevel = 4 # (2)!
}
</code></pre> <ol> <li>The render setting: the level of every subdivision mesh.</li> <li>The per-prim level. It replaces the render setting for this mesh, and on an ancestor for every mesh below it.</li> </ol>
